<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Bagratid Armenia</h1><p class="st-meta">884 CE – 1045 CE · capital Ani</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The medieval Armenian kingdom of the Bagratids, whose capital Ani became famous as the city of a thousand and one churches.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashot-i-of-armenia">Ashot Bagratuni</a>, recognised as prince of princes in 862, received a royal crown from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> around 884 with Byzantine approval, reviving the Armenian monarchy after more than four centuries. The kingdom grew out of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-armenia">Principality of Armenia</a> and the family's vast estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirak">Shirak</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayrarat">Ayrarat</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom was a federation of noble houses and rival kingdoms under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagratuni-dynasty">Bagratid</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negus-nagast">king of kings</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smbat-i-of-armenia">Smbat I</a> was executed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sajid-dynasty">Sajid</a> emir Yusuf in 914, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashot-ii-of-armenia">Ashot II the Iron</a> restored royal power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashot-iii-of-armenia">Ashot III</a> moved the capital to Ani in 961, which grew wealthy on trade between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gagik-i-of-armenia">Gagik I</a> the architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trdat-the-architect">Trdat</a> completed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ani-cathedral">Ani Cathedral</a> in 1001.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hovhannes-smbat-iii">Hovhannes-Smbat</a> bequeathed his kingdom to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a>, and in 1045 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gagik-ii-of-armenia">Gagik II</a> was forced to surrender Ani to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> sacked Ani in 1064, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> moved west towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bagratid-armenia.jpg" alt="Bagratid Armenia" width="320" height="188"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur ·✉·✍· · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bagratuni_Armenia_1000-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>884 CE – 1045 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ani</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ashot I, Ashot III, Gagik I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Armenian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Armenian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/armenia">Armenia</a>, <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Armenia (Bagratid), Kingdom of Ani, Bagratuni Armenia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=964&amp;focus=bagratid-armenia" aria-label="Bagratid Armenia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5749 730 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bagratid Armenia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6069 986L6097 1002M6069 986L6134 1224M6069 986L5709 970M6069 986L6079 1051M6069 986L6057 983M6069 986L6087 970M6069 986L6148 1029"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6097 1002h0M6134 1224h0M5709 970h0M6079 1051h0M6057 983h0M6087 970h0M6148 1029h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6069 98